WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.680
If you had a message to send from Spain to
Norway,

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:06.190
would you call the first relay you could
reach? Or wait for a better one?

00:00:06.458 --> 00:00:08.898
We gave five routing strategies that
choice.

00:00:08.898 --> 00:00:11.738
Let’s follow one message through the
Linkwise simulator,

00:00:11.738 --> 00:00:14.153
then look at what happened across the whole
network.

00:00:14.417 --> 00:00:17.937
We changed the network size, the traffic,
and which links failed.

00:00:17.937 --> 00:00:19.957
With twenty matched seeds for each case,

00:00:19.957 --> 00:00:22.440
that gave us twenty-seven hundred policy
runs.

00:00:22.708 --> 00:00:25.088
Each strategy got the same listening
windows

00:00:25.088 --> 00:00:27.508
and the same underlying radio conditions.

00:00:27.508 --> 00:00:30.616
The queues and collisions depended on who
actually called.

00:00:30.875 --> 00:00:34.655
Here’s message six. It starts in Spain, and
it’s headed for Norway.

00:00:34.655 --> 00:00:36.755
At fifteen oh six, universal time,

00:00:36.755 --> 00:00:39.672
the earliest-arrival router calls a relay
in Britain.

00:00:39.917 --> 00:00:41.497
Britain receives the message.

00:00:41.497 --> 00:00:45.597
But the acknowledgment never makes it back
to Spain. The relay has a copy,

00:00:45.597 --> 00:00:47.911
even though the sender has no confirmation.

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:50.447
Now rewind to the same minute. This time,

00:00:50.447 --> 00:00:54.687
the router weighs a propagation forecast
alongside its own call history.

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.400
It lets the British window pass.

00:00:56.667 --> 00:01:00.287
We chose this message because it’s the
first one in seed seventeen

00:01:00.287 --> 00:01:03.873
where the two strategies ended up with
different delivery outcomes.

00:01:04.125 --> 00:01:06.385
Ten minutes later, Spain calls France.

00:01:06.385 --> 00:01:10.365
The colors on the globe come from an
ionospheric grid for fifteen fifteen,

00:01:10.365 --> 00:01:11.779
one minute before the call.

00:01:12.042 --> 00:01:17.282
Midway along the path, the grid puts the F2
peak at 267 kilometers.

00:01:17.282 --> 00:01:21.362
The vertical electron content there is 25.4
TEC units.

00:01:21.362 --> 00:01:24.406
That’s weather context for a simulated
transmission.

00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:29.067
Remember the copy in Britain? At fifteen
forty, the relay sends it on to Norway.

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:31.254
The handoff is complete one minute later.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.700
In the other run, the combined strategy
never gets message six to Norway.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.259
At fifteen fifty-five, its deadline passes.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.700
You can follow the same events in the
terminal. Read down the left tmux pane:

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.100
first the collisions, then the lost
acknowledgment,

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.005
and finally the transfer to Norway.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:51.090
On the right, the last call fails. These
are the simulator’s own records,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.565
with the same starting conditions on both
sides.

00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:58.373
One message tells only part of the story.
Under light traffic in Europe,

00:01:58.373 --> 00:02:03.517
forecast plus history delivered 72.5
percent after weighting messages by
priority.

00:02:03.792 --> 00:02:08.892
Raise the traffic to the commissioned load
and its score drops to 26.8 percent.

00:02:08.892 --> 00:02:12.640
Earliest arrival delivers 32.1 percent
under that same load.

00:02:12.917 --> 00:02:16.597
Were there enough openings for all six
messages? In this run, yes.

00:02:16.597 --> 00:02:20.837
An offline search found a schedule that got
every message through without a

00:02:20.837 --> 00:02:21.538
radio conflict.

00:02:21.792 --> 00:02:25.732
The best online router delivered one. The
search had a big advantage:

00:02:25.732 --> 00:02:28.093
it could see in advance which calls would
succeed.

00:02:28.333 --> 00:02:32.873
Now pull back to a thousand simulated
stations. With open listening windows,

00:02:32.873 --> 00:02:37.113
every strategy finished the commissioned
workload without delivering a single

00:02:37.113 --> 00:02:37.184
message.

00:02:37.458 --> 00:02:40.938
We ran it again with one caller assigned to
each receiver window.

00:02:40.938 --> 00:02:45.023
The globe is following one message that
made it through under those reservations.

00:02:45.292 --> 00:02:47.872
Over twenty seeds, reservations took
earliest arrival

00:02:47.872 --> 00:02:51.752
from zero deliveries to seven, out of
twenty thousand offers.

00:02:51.752 --> 00:02:54.619
So the gain was real in the simulator, and
small.

00:02:54.875 --> 00:02:56.995
Here’s the bottleneck. In four hours,

00:02:56.995 --> 00:03:01.595
only twenty-four of the nine hundred and
ninety-four access stations got a turn.

00:03:01.595 --> 00:03:03.335
Most never had the chance to call.

00:03:03.583 --> 00:03:07.063
We also put two actual modem processes on a
virtual cable.

00:03:07.063 --> 00:03:11.643
All five clean trials and all five noise-
only trials confirmed the payload.

00:03:11.643 --> 00:03:16.070
An echo or a severe fade on the return path
stopped setup in every trial.

00:03:16.333 --> 00:03:20.293
In a separate fading test, one of five
quiet-channel trials connected,

00:03:20.293 --> 00:03:24.231
and none confirmed the message. On the
disturbed channel, none connected.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.800
Finally, we replayed more than a thousand
seconds of recorded HF audio.

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.820
Two decoders agreed on eighteen valid
receptions,

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:34.217
representing sixteen distinct frame hashes.

00:03:34.458 --> 00:03:37.298
We don’t have transmitter logs for those
recordings.

00:03:37.298 --> 00:03:41.018
To find out whether these routing choices
help on real HF paths,

00:03:41.018 --> 00:03:45.311
we need both ends of each call, on sites
and dates held back from development.
